# Appendix: AR deductions:



000. | Ratio Chasing to prove CI:CJ = CJ:CI:
| Premise CJ = CI gives a linear equation with coefficients {CI²: 1, CJ²: -1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {CI²: 2, CJ²: -2} that gives CI:CJ = CJ:CI



005. | Angle Chasing to prove ∠(CD,CI) = ∠(CJ,CD):
| Premise ∠(CI,IJ) = ∠(IJ,CJ) gives a linear equation with coefficients {CI: -1, CJ: -1, IJ: 2}
| Premise AD ∥ AC gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC: 1, AD: -1}
| Then {CI: 1, CJ: 1, IJ: -2} + 2 x {AC: 1, AD: -1}
|    = {CI: 1, CJ: 1, IJ: -2, AC: 2, AD: -2}
| Premise CD ∥ AD gives a linear equation with coefficients {AD: 1, CD: -1}
| Then {CI: 1, CJ: 1, IJ: -2, AC: 2, AD: -2} + 2 x {AD: 1, CD: -1}
|    = {CI: 1, CJ: 1, IJ: -2, AC: 2, CD: -2}
| Premise AC ⟂ IJ gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC: 1, IJ: -1}
| Then {CI: 1, CJ: 1, IJ: -2, AC: 2, CD: -2} + -2 x {AC: 1, IJ: -1}
|    = {CI: 1, CJ: 1, CD: -2}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {CI: 1, CJ: 1, CD: -2} that gives ∠(CD,CI) = ∠(CJ,CD)



006. | Ratio Chasing to prove CD:CD = CJ:CI:
| Premise CJ = CI gives a linear equation with coefficients {CI²: 1, CJ²: -1}
