010. | Angle Chasing to prove ∠(BI,IJ) = ∠(DE,DJ):
| Premise ∠(CD,DI) = ∠(DJ,CD) gives a linear equation with coefficients {CD: -2, DI: 1, DJ: 1}
| Premise AD ∥ AC gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC: 1, AD: -1}
| Then {CD: 2, DI: -1, DJ: -1} + 2 x {AC: 1, AD: -1}
|    = {CD: 2, DI: -1, DJ: -1, AC: 2, AD: -2}
| Premise BI ∥ DI gives a linear equation with coefficients {BI: 1, DI: -1}
| Then {CD: 2, DI: -1, DJ: -1, AC: 2, AD: -2} + -1 x {BI: 1, DI: -1}
|    = {CD: 2, DJ: -1, AC: 2, AD: -2, BI: -1}
| Premise CD ∥ AD gives a linear equation with coefficients {AD: 1, CD: -1}
| Then {CD: 2, DJ: -1, AC: 2, AD: -2, BI: -1} + 2 x {AD: 1, CD: -1}
|    = {DJ: -1, AC: 2, BI: -1}
| Premise AC ⟂ DE gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC: 1, DE: -1}
| Then {DJ: -1, AC: 2, BI: -1} + -1 x {AC: 1, DE: -1}
|    = {DJ: -1, AC: 1, BI: -1, DE: 1}
| Premise AC ⟂ IJ gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC: 1, IJ: -1}
| Then {DJ: -1, AC: 1, BI: -1, DE: 1} + -1 x {AC: 1, IJ: -1}
|    = {DJ: -1, BI: -1, DE: 1, IJ: 1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {DJ: -1, BI: -1, DE: 1, IJ: 1} that gives ∠(BI,IJ) = ∠(DE,DJ)



011. | Ratio Chasing to prove AB:AC = AI:AI:
| Premise AB = AC gives a linear equation with coefficients {AB²: 1, AC²: -1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {AB²: 1, AC²: -1} that gives AB:AC = AI:AI



015. | Ratio Chasing to prove CI:GI = GI:CI:
| Premise BI = GI gives a linear equation with coefficients {BI²: 1, GI²: -1}
| Premise AI:AI = BI:CI gives a linear equation with coefficients {BI²: -1, CI²: 1}
| Then {BI²: 2, GI²: -2} + 2 x {BI²: -1, CI²: 1}
|    = {GI²: -2, CI²: 2}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {GI²: -2, CI²: 2} that gives CI:GI = GI:CI



019. | Angle Chasing to prove ∠(CG,CJ) = ∠(DI,DE):
| Premise ∠(AB,BC) = ∠(BC,AC) gives a linear equation with coefficients {AB: -1, AC: -1, BC: 2}
| Premise ∠(AB,BI) = ∠(BI,BC) gives a linear equation with coefficients {AB: -1, BC: -1, BI: 2}
| Then {AB: 1/4, AC: 1/4, BC: -1/2} + 1/4 x {AB: -1, BC: -1, BI: 2}
|    = {AC: 1/4, BC: -3/4, BI: 1/2}
| Premise ∠(AC,CI) = ∠(CI,BC) gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC: -1, BC: -1, CI: 2}
| Then {AC: 1/4, BC: -3/4, BI: 1/2} + -3/4 x {AC: -1, BC: -1, CI: 2}
|    = {AC: 1, BI: 1/2, CI: -3/2}
| Premise ∠(CG,CI) = ∠(GI,CG) gives a linear equation with coefficients {CG: -2, CI: 1, GI: 1}
| Then {AC: 1, BI: 1/2, CI: -3/2} + 1/2 x {CG: -2, CI: 1, GI: 1}
|    = {AC: 1, BI: 1/2, CI: -1, CG: -1, GI: 1/2}
| Premise ∠(CI,IJ) = ∠(IJ,CJ) gives a linear equation with coefficients {CI: -1, CJ: -1, IJ: 2}
| Then {AC: 1, BI: 1/2, CI: -1, CG: -1, GI: 1/2} + -1 x {CI: -1, CJ: -1, IJ: 2}
|    = {AC: 1, BI: 1/2, CG: -1, GI: 1/2, CJ: 1, IJ: -2}
| Premise BI ∥ DI gives a linear equation with coefficients {BI: 1, DI: -1}
| Then {AC: 1, BI: 1/2, CG: -1, GI: 1/2, CJ: 1, IJ: -2} + -1 x {BI: 1, DI: -1}
|    = {AC: 1, BI: -1/2, CG: -1, GI: 1/2, CJ: 1, IJ: -2, DI: 1}
| Premise BI ∥ GI gives a linear equation with coefficients {BI: 1, GI: -1}
| Then {AC: 1, BI: -1/2, CG: -1, GI: 1/2, CJ: 1, IJ: -2, DI: 1} + 1/2 x {BI: 1, GI: -1}
|    = {AC: 1, CG: -1, CJ: 1, IJ: -2, DI: 1}
